    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8dadfa4feb92182ee6a872a78056393944eef4a70fc1c7642e81de29a4a81633",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The zeta height-aspect and primitive-Dirichlet q-aspect arguments share a modular extremal interface: a correlation statistic evaluates Fourier-supported test functions through a convolution identity, and bandlimited Beurling-Selberg majorants or minorants compare that evaluation with a pair count. The q-aspect statistic is controlled to frequencies below two, so the same scaled extremizers may use bandwidth Delta approaching two instead of the zeta argument's unit band, reducing the endpoint loss in the resulting fixed-beta bounds.",
